# Why this pipes a remote script when `file.download` refuses to fetch anything without a
# sha256 — the two answer the same trust question in opposite ways, and the position is
# stated here rather than left for a reader to infer (#601).
#
# `file.download` fetches an **artefact**: a release binary, immutable, at a version the
# plan names. A hash of it is checkable because the bytes never move, and the file is
# usually executed later by something else, long after anyone looked. Refusing an unverified
# one costs nothing.
#
# `https://get.docker.com` is a **script that changes with every Docker release**. Pinning a
# hash would break the def on each of them, and a hash bumped without reading the diff is a
# ritual, not a verification — the appearance of the guarantee `file.download` actually
# provides. What is trusted here is TLS and the domain, which is the same trust the
# alternative would need anyway: the apt route means adding Docker's repository and its
# signing key, on the authority of the same domain.
#
# So this def is a **convenience, not a guarantee**. A plan that does not want that trust
# does not call it: it installs docker through apt with a repository and a key it chose,
# which shellf can express with the defs it already has.
#
# The cost is recorded in `test/e2e/def-coverage.sh`: this is the one def no e2e run
# exercises, so the least verified instruction in the stdlib is also the one running remote
# code as root. That is the price of the position above, and it is why the position is
# written down rather than assumed.
def install() as root {
    observe { return state(installed: shell { command -v docker }.exit == 0) }
    apply {
        r = shell { curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh }
        if !r { return err.runtime(r) }
        return ok.installed
    }
}
